</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>The Proposed Method</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section details the proposed method.
We begin by deriving the analytical transition probability for GFMs, and then introduce RL training and refinement in Graph-GRPO.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Estimation of Rate Matrix</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In each denoising step of GFMs, we need to estimate a rate matrix <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to transform the graph from <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{t+\mathrm{d}t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{t+\mathrm{d}t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The marginal distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the rate matrix <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are connected by the Kolmogorov forward equation:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}p_{t}=R_{t}^{\top}p_{t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}p_{t}=R_{t}^{\top}p_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Satisfying the above condition is challenging because it is difficult to directly obtain the differentiation of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
On the other hand, if we can access the real data <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, it is possible to give the conditional differentiation:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}p_{t\mid 1}(z_{t}\mid z_{1})=\delta(z_{t},z_{1})-p_{0}(z_{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}p_{t\mid 1}(z_{t}\mid z_{1})=\delta(z_{t},z_{1})-p_{0}(z_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conditional Rate Matrix.</span>
Given a clean state <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campbell et al. (2024)</a></cite> gives a valid conditional rate matrix:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t}(z_{t},z_{t+\mathrm{d}t}|z_{1})=\frac{\mathrm{ReLU}\!\left[\partial_{t}p_{t\mid 1}(z_{t+\mathrm{d}t}|z_{1})-\partial_{t}p_{t\mid 1}(z_{t}|z_{1})\right]}{Z_{t}^{&gt;0}\cdot p_{t\mid 1}(z_{t}|z_{1})}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mpadded style="width:2.555em;" width="2.555em"><mi mathsize="0.900em">ReLU</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em">∂</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">∂</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">Z</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="0.900em">&gt;</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t}(z_{t},z_{t+\mathrm{d}t}|z_{1})=\frac{\mathrm{ReLU}\!\left[\partial_{t}p_{t\mid 1}(z_{t+\mathrm{d}t}|z_{1})-\partial_{t}p_{t\mid 1}(z_{t}|z_{1})\right]}{Z_{t}^{&gt;0}\cdot p_{t\mid 1}(z_{t}|z_{1})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{&gt;0}_{t}=\left|\{z_{t}:p_{t|1}(z_{t}|z_{1})&gt;0\}\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{&gt;0}_{t}=\left|\{z_{t}:p_{t|1}(z_{t}|z_{1})&gt;0\}\right|</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of states with non-zero probability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Since the ground-truth state <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is unknown during generation, existing GFMs employ a Monte Carlo sampling to sample a pseudo-graph as an alternative to the real data.
As the number of samples increases, the conditional rate matrix gradually transforms into the unconditional rate matrix:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R_{t}(z_{t},z_{t+\mathrm{d}t})=\mathbb{E}_{\hat{z}_{1}\sim p_{\theta}(\cdot|z_{t})}\left[R_{t}(z_{t},z_{t+\mathrm{d}t}|\hat{z}_{1})\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t}(z_{t},z_{t+\mathrm{d}t})=\mathbb{E}_{\hat{z}_{1}\sim p_{\theta}(\cdot|z_{t})}\left[R_{t}(z_{t},z_{t+\mathrm{d}t}|\hat{z}_{1})\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the pseudo-state sampled from <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\theta}(\cdot|z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\cdot|z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. The transition process of GFMs is shown in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.2 Training and Sampling ‣ 2 Preliminary ‣ Graph-GRPO: Training Graph Flow Models with Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Although effective, the conditional rate matrix is not suitable for RL training for two reasons:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Non-differentiable transition probability.</span> The RL optimization relies on calculating a ratio between the old and new policy models, <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(G_{t+\mathrm{d}t}|G_{t})}{\pi_{\text{old}}(G_{t+\mathrm{d}t}|G_{t})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(G_{t+\mathrm{d}t}|G_{t})}{\pi_{\text{old}}(G_{t+\mathrm{d}t}|G_{t})}</annotation></semantics></math>, which requires the policy model <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> to be fully differentiable. However, the conditional rate matrix relies on non-differentiable Monte Carlo sampling, which breaks the gradient flow.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mismatch between training and inference.</span> Even though we can use some tricks, <em id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span>, Gumbel-Softmax <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jang et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, to keep the gradient continuous, there is a more serious mismatch issue in the conditional rate matrix. That is, the new and old models may sample different pseudo-graphs, resulting in inconsistency between the training and inference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analytical Rate Matrix.</span>
To enable stable RL training, we theoretically derive the analytical expression of the rate matrix, which is fully differentiable and aligns the training and inference processes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given the current state <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, current time <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, prior distribution <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and model prediction <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\theta}(\cdot|z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\cdot|z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, the analytic rate matrix is defined as:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R_{t}^{\theta}(z_{t},z_{t+\mathrm{d}t})=p_{\theta}(z_{t+\mathrm{d}t})V_{1}+(1-p_{\theta}(z_{t})-p_{\theta}(z_{t+\mathrm{d}t}))V_{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">V</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">V</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R_{t}^{\theta}(z_{t},z_{t+\mathrm{d}t})=p_{\theta}(z_{t+\mathrm{d}t})V_{1}+(1-p_{\theta}(z_{t})-p_{\theta}(z_{t+\mathrm{d}t}))V_{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{1}=\frac{1\text{+}p_{0}(z_{t})-p_{0}(z_{t+\mathrm{d}t})}{Z_{t}^{&gt;0}(1-t)p_{0}(z_{t})},\ V_{2}=\frac{\mathrm{ReLU}(p_{0}(z_{t})-p_{0}(z_{t+\mathrm{d}t}))}{Z_{t}^{&gt;0}(1-t)p_{0}(z_{t})}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">V</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.900em">+</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">Z</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="0.900em">&gt;</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.627em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">V</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">ReLU</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">Z</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="0.900em">&gt;</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{1}=\frac{1\text{+}p_{0}(z_{t})-p_{0}(z_{t+\mathrm{d}t})}{Z_{t}^{&gt;0}(1-t)p_{0}(z_{t})},\ V_{2}=\frac{\mathrm{ReLU}(p_{0}(z_{t})-p_{0}(z_{t+\mathrm{d}t}))}{Z_{t}^{&gt;0}(1-t)p_{0}(z_{t})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are two statistics that can be pre-calculated before generation.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">See Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Derivation of Discrete Flow Matching for Graphs ‣ Graph-GRPO: Training Graph Flow Models with Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 3.1. ‣ 3.1 Estimation of Rate Matrix ‣ 3 The Proposed Method ‣ Graph-GRPO: Training Graph Flow Models with Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> states that the rate matrix can be directly estimated from the predictions of the denoiser by traversing all possibilities of the real data.
As a result, the denoiser can be optimized by the policy gradient, and the reward can be maximized.
The transition process of Graph-GRPO is shown in Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 2.2 Training and Sampling ‣ 2 Preliminary ‣ Graph-GRPO: Training Graph Flow Models with Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.10395v1/method1.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/214;" width="476" height="214" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The overall framework of Graph-GRPO. (1) <span id="S3.F2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rollout</span>: given a noisy graph, the policy model samples <math id="S3.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> denoising trajectories and caches the graph state <math id="S3.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t+\Delta t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t+\Delta t}</annotation></semantics></math> along with its transition probability <math id="S3.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t+\Delta t}^{\text{old}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mtext>old</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t+\Delta t}^{\text{old}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the meantime, we normalize the reward scores to calculate the advantage of each trajectory. (2) <span id="S3.F2.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RL training</span>: we select a graph <math id="S3.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from the rollouts and use the new policy model to estimate the transition probabilities <math id="S3.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t+\Delta t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t+\Delta t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Graph-GRPO maximizes the rewards by optimizing the advantage-weighted ratio in Eq. <a href="#S3.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ 3.2 Graph-GRPO ‣ 3 The Proposed Method ‣ Graph-GRPO: Training Graph Flow Models with Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Graph-GRPO</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce the pipeline of Graph-GRPO. The framework is shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Estimation of Rate Matrix ‣ 3 The Proposed Method ‣ Graph-GRPO: Training Graph Flow Models with Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rollout Collection.</span>
A rollout refers to a trajectory <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> generated by sequentially sampling actions from the policy model <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which can be formulated as:</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=\{s_{0},a_{0},\cdots,s_{T},a_{T}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=\{s_{0},a_{0},\cdots,s_{T},a_{T}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> denotes state, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> represents an action, and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the length of the trajectory.
In our context, the policy model is a pre-trained GFM, the state is the input graph, and the action samples the nodes and edges of the new graph.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To implement group-relative optimization, we first need to collect a set of trajectories starting from the same origin, <em id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, a noisy graph <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{0}\sim p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{0}\sim p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Conditioned on the same noise graph, GFMs will generate a group of independent trajectories <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\tau^{(k)}\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\tau^{(k)}\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> in parallel, where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the group size.
During this process, we explicitly record the sampled trajectory <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{(k)}=\{G_{0},G_{\Delta t}^{(k)},\cdots,G_{1-\Delta t}^{(k)},G_{1}^{(k)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{(k)}=\{G_{0},G_{\Delta t}^{(k)},\cdots,G_{1-\Delta t}^{(k)},G_{1}^{(k)}\}</annotation></semantics></math> along with the transition probability <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(G^{(k)}_{t+\Delta t}|G^{(k)}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(G^{(k)}_{t+\Delta t}|G^{(k)}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> at each step.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Upon reaching the terminal state <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math>, we evaluate the final graph to compute the reward <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{(k)}=\mathcal{R}(G_{1}^{(k)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{(k)}=\mathcal{R}(G_{1}^{(k)})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the task-specific reward functions, <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, structural validity or docking scores.
Subsequently, we utilize the cached trajectory <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math>, the sequence of step-wise transition probabilities, and the reward <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math> to compute the advantage and the importance sampling ratio for RL training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RL Training.</span>
The goal of RL is to learn an optimal policy that maximizes the expected reward function.
Given the pre-collected rollouts, we aim to learn a new policy model <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> that achieves higher rewards than the old policy model <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{old}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{old}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be formulated as:</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi_{\theta}}\left[R(\tau)\right]=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi_{\text{old}}}\left[\frac{p_{\theta}(\tau)}{p_{\text{old}}(\tau)}R(\tau)\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi_{\theta}}\left[R(\tau)\right]=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi_{\text{old}}}\left[\frac{p_{\theta}(\tau)}{p_{\text{old}}(\tau)}R(\tau)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{p_{\theta}(\tau)}{p_{\text{old}}(\tau)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{p_{\theta}(\tau)}{p_{\text{old}}(\tau)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the importance sampling that performs a change of measure between rollout distributions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In the framework of Graph-GRPO, we use the standard Group Relative Policy Optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> for RL training. The objective function is defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\frac{1}{KT}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\left(\mathcal{L}_{t}^{(k)}(\theta)-\beta D_{\text{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}\|\pi_{\text{ref}})\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\frac{1}{KT}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\left(\mathcal{L}_{t}^{(k)}(\theta)-\beta D_{\text{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}\|\pi_{\text{ref}})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{t}^{(i)}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{t}^{(i)}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the policy optimization in the <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th rollout and <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th denoising step, <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperparameter, and <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\text{KL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{KL}}</annotation></semantics></math> is used to prevent the RL-optimized model <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> from drastically deviating from the base model.
The loss of policy optimization is formulated as follows.</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{t}^{(k)}(\theta)=\min\left(r_{t,\theta}^{(k)}A^{(k)},\operatorname{clip}(r_{t,\theta}^{(k)},1\pm\epsilon)A^{(k)}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>clip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>±</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{t}^{(k)}(\theta)=\min\left(r_{t,\theta}^{(k)}A^{(k)},\operatorname{clip}(r_{t,\theta}^{(k)},1\pm\epsilon)A^{(k)}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A^{(k)}=\frac{R^{(k)}-\operatorname{mean}(\{R^{(i)}\})}{\operatorname{std}(\{R^{(i)}\})},\ r_{t,\theta}^{(k)}=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(G_{t+\Delta t}^{(k)}|G_{t}^{(k)})}{\pi_{\text{old}}(G_{t+\Delta t}^{(k)}|G_{t}^{(k)})}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>mean</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>std</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A^{(k)}=\frac{R^{(k)}-\operatorname{mean}(\{R^{(i)}\})}{\operatorname{std}(\{R^{(i)}\})},\ r_{t,\theta}^{(k)}=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(G_{t+\Delta t}^{(k)}|G_{t}^{(k)})}{\pi_{\text{old}}(G_{t+\Delta t}^{(k)}|G_{t}^{(k)})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the group-relative advantage and <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t,\theta}^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t,\theta}^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the importance sampling ratio.
Moreover, to prevent reward hacking and preserve chemical validity, we use the KL divergence to penalize deviations.</p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\text{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}\|\pi_{\text{ref}})=\pi_{\theta}(G^{(k)}_{t+\Delta t}|G^{(k)}_{t})\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(G^{(k)}_{t+\Delta t}|G^{(k)}_{t})}{\pi_{\text{ref}}(G^{(k)}_{t+\Delta t}|G^{(k)}_{t})}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}\|\pi_{\text{ref}})=\pi_{\theta}(G^{(k)}_{t+\Delta t}|G^{(k)}_{t})\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(G^{(k)}_{t+\Delta t}|G^{(k)}_{t})}{\pi_{\text{ref}}(G^{(k)}_{t+\Delta t}|G^{(k)}_{t})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the base model, <em id="S3.SS2.p6.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S3.SS2.p6.4.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, a pre-trained DeFoG checkpoint without RL training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Refinement</h3>

<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.10395v1/method2.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/175;" width="476" height="175" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Refinement in Graph-GRPO. We first use GFMs to denoise a noise graph from <math id="S3.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Subsequently, we re-noise the generated graph to time step <math id="S3.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> and denoise it again using GFMs. This strategy explicitly increases the denoising steps of GFMs and improves the generation quality of Graph-GRPO.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In standard sampling workflows, GFMs aim to progressively transform the noisy graph <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> into a clean graph <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a de novo generation process.
However, real-world applications require graphs with specific properties, which may only exist within a small region of the entire generative space.
In this case, de novo graph generation will result in invalid or low-quality graphs, which cannot effectively explore the high-potential region in the chemical space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this issue, we propose a refinement strategy, which explores the potential region by iteratively renoising and regenerating promising graphs. An example is shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3 Refinement ‣ 3 The Proposed Method ‣ Graph-GRPO: Training Graph Flow Models with Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
Specifically, we maintain a priority pool <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> that stores the graphs with top-<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> reward scores.
In each iteration, these candidate graphs undergo a refinement cycle, which consists of two distinct phases:</p>
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Renoising.</span>
For a candidate graph <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}\in\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}\in\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, we revert it to an intermediate noisy state at time <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\epsilon}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\epsilon}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> based on the conditional probability path:</p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t_{\epsilon}|1}(z_{t_{\epsilon}}|z_{1})=t_{\epsilon}\cdot\delta(z_{t_{\epsilon}},z_{1})+(1-t_{\epsilon})\cdot p_{0}(z_{t_{\epsilon}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t_{\epsilon}|1}(z_{t_{\epsilon}}|z_{1})=t_{\epsilon}\cdot\delta(z_{t_{\epsilon}},z_{1})+(1-t_{\epsilon})\cdot p_{0}(z_{t_{\epsilon}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> controls the perturbation level: a larger <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> means a smaller noise amplitude, thus enabling controlled exploration.
In practice, we typically sample multiple noisy graphs, <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{t_{\epsilon}}\sim p(z_{t_{\epsilon}}|z_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{t_{\epsilon}}\sim p(z_{t_{\epsilon}}|z_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, to explore the potential of <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Regeneration.</span>
Given a set of noisy graphs, we re-execute the denoising process of GFMs to generate more candidates.
Subsequently, we evaluate the reward score of new graphs, compare them with the graphs in the priority queue, and retain <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> graphs with the highest scores.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">More technical details can be seen in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Dynamic Prior and Graph Size Adjustment ‣ Graph-GRPO: Training Graph Flow Models with Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiment</h2>

