</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Reward Details</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following previous works and standard evaluation benchmarks, we establish a unified protocol for handling generated graphs with disconnected components.
Specifically, for the <span id="A5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Protein Docking</span> task, we extract the largest connected component (LCC) of the generated graph prior to scoring.
For <span id="A5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Target Property Generation</span> tasks, we directly utilize the standard oracle functions provided by the PMO framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2022</a>)</cite> to calculate rewards.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Protein Docking Rewards</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the protein docking optimization task, we employ a composite reward function consisting of four terms, each strictly bounded in <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. The detailed definitions are as follows:</p>
<ul id="A5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Drug-likeness (<math id="A5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\text{QED}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">QED</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\text{QED}}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> This component ensures basic drug-like properties, defined as an indicator function <math id="A5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}(\text{QED}(G)&gt;0.5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>QED</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}(\text{QED}(G)&gt;0.5)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Synthetic Accessibility (<math id="A5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\text{SA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">SA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\text{SA}}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> This represents the normalized synthetic accessibility score, calculated as <math id="A5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(10-\text{SA}(G))/9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>SA</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(10-\text{SA}(G))/9</annotation></semantics></math>. It favors molecules that are easier to synthesize (lower SA values).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Novelty (<math id="A5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\text{Nov}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">Nov</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\text{Nov}}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> This term encourages exploration beyond the training distribution <math id="A5.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is computed as <math id="A5.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\max_{G^{\prime}\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}}\text{Sim}(G,G^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mtext>Sim</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\max_{G^{\prime}\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}}\text{Sim}(G,G^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <span id="A5.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Sim</span> denotes the Tanimoto similarity of Morgan fingerprints.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Docking Score (<math id="A5.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\text{DS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">DS</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\text{DS}}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> This is the normalized docking score derived from the binding energy <math id="A5.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> (kcal/mol), formulated as <math id="A5.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R_{\text{DS}}=\text{Clip}(-E/20,0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>DS</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext>Clip</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\text{DS}}=\text{Clip}(-E/20,0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, where lower energy corresponds to a higher reward.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Early Pruning.</span> To improve training efficiency, we also implement the early-pruning strategy from GDPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: the computationally expensive docking simulation (<math id="A5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\text{DS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>DS</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\text{DS}}</annotation></semantics></math>) is performed only for molecules that satisfy the drug-likeness constraint (<math id="A5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\text{QED}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>QED</mtext></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\text{QED}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>); otherwise, <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\text{DS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>DS</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\text{DS}}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 0.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Target Property Optimization Rewards</h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the <span id="A5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Valsartan SMARTS</span> task, the objective is to generate molecules containing a specific substructure while matching the physicochemical properties of a reference molecule (Sitagliptin). The total reward <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the geometric mean of a binary substructure score <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{struct}}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>struct</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{struct}}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and three physicochemical property scores <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{s_{1},s_{2},s_{3}\}\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{s_{1},s_{2},s_{3}\}\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A5.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(G)=\left(s_{\text{struct}}\cdot s_{1}\cdot s_{2}\cdot s_{3}\right)^{\frac{1}{4}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>struct</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(G)=\left(s_{\text{struct}}\cdot s_{1}\cdot s_{2}\cdot s_{3}\right)^{\frac{1}{4}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The property scores <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are calculated using Gaussian kernels centered at the reference values, which decay exponentially as the molecule’s properties deviate from the target.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The Sparse Reward Challenge.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This geometric mean formulation creates a severe optimization bottleneck during the cold-start phase. Even in the rare event that the model accidentally generates a valid scaffold (<math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{struct}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>struct</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{struct}}=1</annotation></semantics></math>), the associated physicochemical properties typically deviate significantly from the specific targets of Sitagliptin. Due to the rapid decay of Gaussian kernels, the property scores <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> drop to near-zero values. Consequently, the multiplicative nature of the geometric mean suppresses the total reward <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(G)</annotation></semantics></math> to a negligible magnitude (e.g., <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>). This results in an extremely weak gradient signal that is easily drowned out by training noise, preventing the model from reinforcing these valid structural discoveries.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Solution: Two-Stage Training Curriculum.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To address the vanishing gradient issue, we implement a curriculum strategy. We emphasize that this is strictly a training-time scheduling technique designed to bootstrap the policy, and is distinct from the refinement strategy used during inference. The training process is divided into two phases:</p>
<ol id="A5.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training Stage 1 (Structural Warm-up):</span> We first optimize the policy using a simplified objective that focuses solely on the binary substructure constraint (<math id="A5.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=s_{\text{struct}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>struct</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=s_{\text{struct}}</annotation></semantics></math>). Unlike the geometric mean, this provides a clear, non-vanishing gradient signal, enabling the policy to quickly learn the topological requirements of the target scaffold.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training Stage 2 (Multi-Objective Training):</span> We use the checkpoint saved from Stage 1 to initialize the full RL training. With the policy already capable of generating the correct scaffold, the geometric mean reward becomes non-zero (<math id="A5.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>). This allows the RL algorithm to effectively optimize the remaining physicochemical property scores.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Additional Results</h2>

<section id="A6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.1 </span>Protein Docking Metrics.</h3>

<div id="A6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A6.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ F.1 Protein Docking Metrics. ‣ Appendix F Additional Results ‣ Graph-GRPO: Training Graph Flow Models with Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> details the generative metrics for the five target proteins.
The results show that Graph-GRPO maintains near-perfect validity and uniqueness (<math id="A6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\%</annotation></semantics></math>) while optimizing for high binding affinities.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A6.T6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 6</span>: </span><span id="A6.T6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Detailed performance metrics of Graph-GRPO on five distinct targets.</span></figcaption>
<div id="A6.T6.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:207.0pt;height:40.8pt;vertical-align:-19.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-81.8pt,16.2pt) scale(0.558534073110909,0.558534073110909) ;">
<table id="A6.T6.5.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A6.T6.5.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A6.T6.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="A6.T6.5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Target</span></td>
<td id="A6.T6.5.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="A6.T6.5.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Valid.</span></td>
<td id="A6.T6.5.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="A6.T6.5.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Uniq.</span></td>
<td id="A6.T6.5.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="A6.T6.5.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Nov.</span></td>
<td id="A6.T6.5.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="A6.T6.5.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Avg. QED</span></td>
<td id="A6.T6.5.1.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="A6.T6.5.1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Avg. SA</span></td>
<td id="A6.T6.5.1.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="A6.T6.5.1.1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Avg. Reward</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A6.T6.5.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A6.T6.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="A6.T6.5.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">parp1</span></td>
<td id="A6.T6.5.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">97.803</td>
<td id="A6.T6.5.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">100.000</td>
<td id="A6.T6.5.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">0.945</td>
<td id="A6.T6.5.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">0.667</td>
<td id="A6.T6.5.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">0.655</td>
<td id="A6.T6.5.1.2.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">0.459</td></tr>
<tr id="A6.T6.5.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A6.T6.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="A6.T6.5.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fa7</span></td>
<td id="A6.T6.5.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">98.617</td>
<td id="A6.T6.5.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">100.000</td>
<td id="A6.T6.5.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">0.992</td>
<td id="A6.T6.5.1.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">0.726</td>
<td id="A6.T6.5.1.3.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">0.650</td>
<td id="A6.T6.5.1.3.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">0.358</td></tr>
<tr id="A6.T6.5.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
